 that came out from that  last year in molecular biology and  evolution again just figuring out  automated ways of reconstructing  transmission events just from genomic  alone so a really powerful tool for  understanding TB epidemiology even if  you can't necessarily get out there in  the community and ask so I'll finish  with one very quick story of paper that  we published earlier this year in ji D  looking at pediatric TB cases TB in  children in BC so we had  over that study retrospective study  period there were nearly 100 cases of  pediatric TB and we had sequence of all  material for 1/2 of those we also had  lots of really interesting information  like where was that child born where  were their parents born if they were  born outside of Canada or their parents  were born outside of Canada when did  they immigrate to the country that they  have a travel history of going back to  say their parents birth country for  extended vacation and we have this large  data set of all these genotypes and all  these genome sequencing results so what  we found when we use genomics to answer  the question where did children in  British Columbia get their TB from as we  found it was very dependent on how old  that child was and where they and their  parents were born so in the subsequent  slides the color scheme here is going to  be a kind of burgundy to represent  children that were born in Canada to  parents who were also born in Canada the  pink bar is lighter pink are children  that were born in Canada whose parents  were born outside Canada and blue are  children that were not born in Canada  they were born overseas so in the age  distribution of cases you can see that  Canadian born cases tend to be younger  foreign-born cases of active TB tend to  be teenagers and where contact  investigation and field epidemiology had  suggested a potential source of a  child's TB whole genome sequencing  confirmed that so in our Canadian born  children with Canadian born parents it  was often visitors to the home community  members or sometimes adult household  contacts for almost all of our children  that were Canadian born with  foreign-born parents it was an adult  household contact often a parent that  genomics indicated was the source and we  did have one child that was born  overseas who was transmitted to NBC from  an adult household contact so in total  of the 49 pediatric cases that we  negated whole genome sequencing  suggested that 15 of those were the  result of transmission in BC and the  rest of those are kind of buried  we had canadian-born children whose  parents were born overseas and those  kids traveled back to those countries so  they probably acquired their TV abroad  we believe that almost all of the  foreign-born children acquired the TB  before they emigrated to Canada now  there are a couple of mysteries too so  genomics can't solve everything we can  only sequence the isolates that we have  access to which in our case were the  ones from BC but it can tell us some  really interesting things and so it to  us that suggested that one of the things  that we do when we diagnose a pediatric  cases TB something called reverse  contact investigation we don't  necessarily need to do that if we see an  older child who was born overseas  because the genome sequencing work tells  us that they probably didn't get their  infection in BC it was probably the  result of acquisition prior to  immigration or as a result of travel so  this is a really small but interesting  example of how genome sequencing can  really change how we do our TB care so  what are we doing next we're still  looking at that retrospective data set  as well as analyzing all of our new data  we've got a very cool project where  we're looking at our transmission  networks we've divided people into  people that transmit and people that  don't transmit and we're combining that  with some linked administrative data we  have to answer some questions like are  there particular clinical or  epidemiological characteristics that  mean that somebody's more likely to  transmit TB than somebody else and are  there features of a small TB outbreak  that would allow us to predict whether  that outbreak is going to grow and  become a particular problem later on so  this is some work that we're doing with  machine learning and predictive  analytics so to sum up TV is a really  interesting organism to work in it's got  a really straightforward genome to  analyze with whole genome sequencing but  we can act and answer a lot of really  important  questions around diagnosis around  treatment around transmission and when  we use that information that we're  deriving from whole genome sequence is  the basis for changing how we do TB  practice to be policy in British  Columbia we really think we can progress  towards elimination so lots of people  contributed to this work including folks  at the BC Center for Disease Control in  the BC Public Health lab collaborators  up in Yukon Territory collaborators at  Oxford University and public health  England and around the world I've seen a  tremendously exciting project to work on  and we're really excited to see where  our TB genome sequencing analysis takes  us next thank you all so much for  joining in and I'm happy to take your  questions please do send them on to me  thanks again  thank you dr. Gardy for that informative  presentation and we will now start the  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar and we'll  address some of the most commonly asked  questions by our viewers  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do - so now just click on the  ask a question drop-down box located on  the far left of your presentation window  type your questions into the box that  appear on the screen and click the send  button our speaker will follow up with  your questions via email  so let's get started our first question  dr. Garvey is what role in genomics play  in higher TB incidence settings yeah  that's an excellent question so the work  that I presented here largely in these  low incidence settings places like BC  and we have very different needs and  very different end goals than a higher  incidence setting so while I think the  priority for high incidence countries  really does need to be on simple rapid  case identification and getting people  on antibiotic therapy right away just  using simple laboratory techniques you  don't need a whole genome sequencing for  that what I think it's going to do is a  couple things I think first of all as we  start to sequence more and more TB in  low incidence settings that's going to  tell us a lot of stuff about the TB  genome that we can use to develop better  rapid non-genomic point-of-care testing  for higher burden settings so we're just  going to be generating a lot of  knowledge that's going to help us do  better Diagnostics for lower and  middle-income countries I think there's  also some interesting things that  genomics can do we know that in certain  high burden countries reporting of TB  cases isn't always perfect the actual  number of TB cases usually far exceeds  the number that are reported to the  government and with some of the  population genomic analyses where we're  using that evolutionary biology and the  math modeling we can actually look at TB  genome sampled from a population and  estimate the true population size you  know we will only sequenced a thousand  TB genomes but based on the diversity we  see how large is the true population  that's out there so I think that can be  quite informative in terms of saying  okay is this country really on target  with their reporting or is there a lot  of undiscovered TB out there that maybe  we need to come up with better  programmatic ways of identifying so  while I don't think whole genome  sequencing is going to become routinely  used in high burden settings any time  soon I think there's a lot that we can  learn from its deployment elsewhere that  can feedback and result in better policy  and practice in  those settings  thank you dr. Gardie and this next  question is actually two parts what  about mixed infections or within hosts  genetic variations how does that affect  your results yeah that's a great  question  so because TB has this latency period  because of the chronic infection and  hangs out in people's lungs for a while  you might expect that the TB bacterium  within an individual the population of  TB bacteria might have a little bit of  variation in there so we've been working  on some methods to account for the fact  that you know what if what if one person  harbors you know two types of TB one  where it's got a mutation at a  particular position and maybe another  where that mutation doesn't exist tell  you then infer who they might have  transmitted to because some people are  going to receive one variant some people  are going to receive the other variant  so we've been working to solve that with  a bunch of different mathematical models  the other question the other parts that  mixed infection we know from genotyping  studies that in high incidence settings  as many as one in five people can be  infected with two different strains of  TB at the same time this doesn't really  happen in lower incidence settings like  British Columbia but it is a problem in  higher burden settings unfortunately  when we do deep whole genome sequencing  we can actually bioinformatics  separate out those two strings we can  identify mixed infections and we can  analyze each one of those populations  separately it's a very very new area of  research there's just been a few papers  on it this year but yeah the sequencing  technology allows us to and if  deconvolute those  thank you dr. Guardian we have one more  question will genomics completely  replace the current protocols in the TV  laboratory yeah  so I think in places like public health  England you've had almost a  near-complete switchover  the only thing that's really still  happening is phenotypic drug sensitivity  testing and with some results that was  part of this large international  consortium have coming out soon I think  in certain settings where the prevalence  of drug-resistant TB is extremely low I  think within two to five years you might  see whole genome sequencing completely  replacing all of the traditional  laboratory steps you would only do  things like phenotypic drug sensitivity  testing if the drug the predicted  resistance of your isolate suggests a  certain treatment regimen but then your  patient isn't responding there's some  sort of empirical evidence that the  treatment isn't working then you might  go in and do phenotypic DST but I think  at certain settings we are looking at a  future where whole genome sequencing  could be it as I said earlier certainly  not in higher burden settings lower  middle-income countries but in certain  state and regional and national  reference laboratories I think it's a  very very real possibility  thank you and dr. Cardy would you like  to provide our viewers with some closing  remarks before we go yeah absolutely  this is one one little slice of a  fascinating world of Public Health  genomics I think TB is one of the areas  where because the TB genome is  relatively straightforward and because  it's such a critically important  pathogen in terms of global Public  Health it's certainly being one of the  ones that has been most studied with the  genomic approach and we've made a lot of  really interesting theoretical and  practical ground work here but the stuff  that we've done in TB is totally  extensible to other pathogens and as  we're starting to you know as a Public  Health and microbiology community really  trying to get a handle on what it seems  like sort of increasing numbers of  epidemics you know we've got  hospital-acquired outbreaks and  superbugs left right and center  foodborne illness outbreaks happening  regularly emerging infectious diseases  in the viral space more and more Ebola  outbreaks zika virus all of these sorts  of techniques are applicable across that  array of pathogens so it's a really  exciting time to be somebody who's able  to combine bacteriology virology with  genomics with computational biology and  with epidemiology and I think it's going  to be one of the most interesting and  most productive areas of biomedical  research in the coming year so is it  it's a great place to work you'll never  be without a job thank you so much dr.  Garvey for your presentation and your  important research we also like to thank  lab roots for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  remind everyone that today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  through December of 2018 you'll receive  an email from lab roots letting you know  when this webcast will be available for  replay please share this announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's event that's all for now and  thanks for joining us hope to see you  again soon bye bye
 

